# --- s059 Book I d.21
n("n920","s059-01","vtrum ista propositio sit vera. solus pater est deus","la","source","Corresponds to Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 21 q. un. (on whether *solus Pater est Deus* is true).","to-verify")
n("n921","s059-01","Aug. 6. de trini. c. 8.","la","intertext","Augustine, *De Trin.* VI 7.9–9.10, where Augustine denies that the Father alone is God. Gorris's chapter number reflects a medieval division. The passage is cited by Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 21.","to-verify")
n("n922","s059-02","adducentes illud prisciani","la","intertext","Priscian, *Institutiones grammaticae*, presumably on *solus* as an exclusive. The passage is not identified (Scotus's lemma).","to-verify")
n("n923","s059-02","Guillermo garronis in. 3°. suo di. 7. q. vnica.","la","intertext","‘Garronis’ is the same William of Ware called *Varronis* in s057 (n895); the variant spelling is kept. The meaning of *in 3° suo* (‘his third’ — book III? or a scribal slip?) is unclear, as is how a d. 21 topic sits in ‘d. 7’. The reference may be to *In III Sent.* d. 7 (on the Incarnation, where propositions like *solus* are also discussed). Not checked.","uncertain")
n("n924","s059-02","sanctus Bonauentura dubio. 3°. literali quarte distinctionis huius primi","la","intertext","Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* d. 4, *dubia circa litteram*, dub. 3 (on *solus Deus Pater*). Lombard's d. 4 raises the question of *solus Pater*-type locutions. The dubium itself has not been checked.","to-verify")
n("n925","s059-04","Egidii li. p°. dis. 21.","la","source","Giles of Rome, *In I Sent.* d. 21.","to-verify")
n("n926","s059-04","relatiua sunt huiusmodi per philosophum in predicamentis","la","intertext","Aristotle, *Cat.* 7, 7b15 (relatives are simultaneous by nature) and 8a35–b15 (whoever knows one relative definitely knows the other).","certain")
n("n927","s059-06","exclusiua affirmatiua infert vniuersalem affirmatiuam de terminis transpositis","la","doctrinal","These are standard rules of the *tractatus de exclusivis* in terminist logic. Compare Peter of Spain, *Syncategoreumata* (ed. de Rijk) on *solus/tantum*.","to-verify")
n("n928","s059-08","vel in illa committitur fallacia:","la","textual","A ends the section with a colon. The sense ‘otherwise a fallacy is committed’ (fallacy of the consequent) is likely.","conjecture")
n("n929","s059-02","his third [book]","en","translation","Latin *in 3° suo*; see Latin note on the reference.")

# --- B collation (full): B78L–B79L
upd("n921", append="B cites only *aug. vi. de trini.* (om. *c. 8*).")
upd("n923", text="B reads *guilhelmo varronis*, so A's *garronis* is a misprint (g for v) for the *Varronis* of s057 (n895), i.e. William of Ware; A's form is kept in the text. B agrees on the reference (*in. iij. suo dis. vij. q. vnica*). The meaning of *in 3° suo* (‘his third’ — book III?) remains unclear, as does how a d. 21 topic sits in ‘d. 7’. The reference may be to *In III Sent.* d. 7 (on the Incarnation, where propositions like *solus* are also discussed). Not checked in Ware.")
n("n1018","s059-06","duas propositiones exponendas","la","textual","B: *duas propositiones exponentes*, the usual technical term (A itself goes on to speak of *illarum exponentium*). A is construable (‘two propositions by which it is to be expounded’) and the English, ‘two propositions to expound it’, fits either reading.","certain")
upd("n928", text="A ends the section with a colon. B has *vel in illa committatur fallacia* followed by a full stop, so nothing is missing; the sense is ‘otherwise a fallacy (of the consequent) would be committed’.", status="verified")
